  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nInteresting upper level pattern beginning to develop for the \nwestern US as the subtropical high relaxes its grip across the \nSilver State. The summertime subtropical ridge that has been \nanchored over the eastern Great Basin is beginning to shift which\nis allowing some monsoonal moisture to work its way into central \nNevada. This will help lower temperatures to more seasonal values \nand with the moisture rain chances due to afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms will be prevalent this week. \n\nTuesday afternoon will have a 20% to 30% chance for thunderstorms\nmainly along the US 50 corridor. These storms will be slow \nmoving, allowing for a higher chances for rain to reach the ground\ngiven the dry surface layer as wet storms may produce up to 0.10\u201d\nof rain. Still there will be a few dry thunderstorms that form, \nwith an elevated risk for lightning fire starts. \n\nWednesday a digging upper level trough will work its way into \nCalifornia, Models show that this system will help Nevada out in \nmultiple ways, first dropping temperatures significantly through \nthe end of the week with highs returning to the low 80s to low \n90s. Second, it will help channel monsoonal moisture flow \nincreasing chances for wetter storm modes. Finally, the added \ndynamic cooling and dynamic lift will also help to increase \noverall precipitation chances for mid to late week. The only \nquestion that is unresolved is how far north and west the best \nmoisture gets advected over NV. Current thinking has all but far \nNW Humboldt County seeing a chance at wetting rains (\u22650.10\u201d) this \nweek. However, ensemble model members are split on this scenario \nas some show all of Humboldt getting in on rain, whereas some show\nmost of N NV missing out. \n\nAfter Saturday the upper trough lifts NE into Canada which may \nlower storm chances a bit for the weekend, but models are split on\nthe upper pattern after Sunday as the GFS favors keeping the \nwestern US under some form of troughing aloft, while the ECMWF \nshows that the upper ridge will again shift back west after a few \ndays in the southern plains. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\nThere is high confidence for chances of isolated to scattered mix\nof wet and dry thunderstorms Through Friday afternoon. \n\nThere moderate confidence for isolated to scattered mix of wet \nand dry thunderstorms for NE Nevada Wednesday through Friday.\n\nThere is low confidence for isolated mix of wet or dry \nthunderstorms for NW Nevada Wednesday through Friday. \n\nThere is low confidence in the evolution of the upper pattern \nafter Sunday as models are disagreement as to the strength and \nlocation of key upper level features.",
